! Index result indicators: the OFF states, and that a load CLEARS.
!
! WHY THIS EXISTS.  ibox1_bind.strap transcribes ja_ax1a's whole
! "BRANCH ON I BOX INDICATORS" routine and pins all five index result
! indicators -- but only ON.  Every group there checks its indicator
! set, and the only OFF any of them checks is the one the trailing-Z
! CLEAR produces, never an indicator off because its condition is
! false.  That was measured, not guessed: a model that sets XF, XCZ,
! XVLZ, XVZ and XVGZ UNCONDITIONALLY TO 1 on every index load passes
! the entire deck routine, and passes index_result_indicators.strap
! too, whose checks are all branch-IF-ON.
!
! So this program is the complement, and it is the control for that
! injection: with the indicators forced on it must FAIL.
!
! WEAKER STANDARD, STATED.  This is HAND-WRITTEN from the Reference
! Manual, not transcribed from a listing -- the deck has no block that
! checks these off.  p.24: an index load sets the index flag and index
! result indicators from the loaded word, so all five are ASSIGNED,
! which means a load must clear the ones whose condition is false.
!
! EVERY OFF-CHECK FOLLOWS AN ON-STATE, which is the whole point.  The
! three words below are the deck's own three seed constants -- IBXWZO,
! IBXWZ2 and IBXWZ1 from ja_ax1a pp.24-25 -- and they walk the value
! negative, positive and zero.  Loading the second after the first
! demands that XF, XCZ and XVLZ be turned OFF, not merely left alone;
! a model that only ever sets bits fails there rather than at the end.
!
! The idiom is the deck's: B<ind>,$ hangs if the bit is ON, BZ<ind>,$
! hangs if the bit is OFF.

        SLC, (8)2200.0
!
! ---- value -1, flag 1, count 0: XF, XCZ, XVLZ on; XVZ, XVGZ off ----
START   LX, $X1, WNEG
        BZXF, $                      ! hang up if XF off
        BZXCZ, $                     ! hang up if XCZ off
        BZXVLZ, $                    ! hang up if XVLZ off
        BXVZ, $                      ! hang up if XVZ on
        BXVGZ, $                     ! hang up if XVGZ on
!
! ---- value +1, flag 0, count 1: only XVGZ on ----------------------
! Everything checked off here was ON a moment ago, so each of these
! four is a test that the load CLEARED it.
        LX, $X1, WPOS
        BXF, $                       ! hang up if XF on
        BXCZ, $                      ! hang up if XCZ on
        BXVLZ, $                     ! hang up if XVLZ on
        BXVZ, $                      ! hang up if XVZ on
        BZXVGZ, $                    ! hang up if XVGZ off
!
! ---- value 0, flag 0, count 1: only XVZ on ------------------------
! XVGZ was on a moment ago and must now be off.
        LX, $X1, WZERO
        BZXVZ, $                     ! hang up if XVZ off
        BXVGZ, $                     ! hang up if XVGZ on
        BXF, $                       ! hang up if XF on
        BXCZ, $                      ! hang up if XCZ on
        BXVLZ, $                     ! hang up if XVLZ on
!
! ---- breaking the flag/count CONFOUND -----------------------------
! The three words above are the deck's own seed constants, and in all
! three FLAG == (COUNT == 0).  That is a confound, and it made every
! index-indicator program here blind to a model reporting XF from the
! COUNT field instead of the flag: a mutation doing exactly that was
! caught 300 sections later by an I/O test, incidentally, and by
! nothing written for the purpose.  These two words vary the fields
! INDEPENDENTLY, one in each direction.
        LX, $X1, WFLAG               ! flag 1, count 1
        BZXF, $                      ! hang up if XF off -- flag is 1
        BXCZ, $                      ! hang up if XCZ on -- count is NOT 0
        LX, $X1, WNOFLG              ! flag 0, count 0
        BXF, $                       ! hang up if XF on  -- flag is 0
        BZXCZ, $                     ! hang up if XCZ off -- count IS 0
!
! ---- a NEGATIVE ZERO value ----------------------------------------
! p.24: XVLZ is set when the value field is "non-zero and negative",
! so a value of minus zero must leave XVLZ OFF and XVZ ON.  Nothing
! reached this: dropping the non-zero term survived the whole suite.
! The word is a raw DD literal because the assembler normalises -0 in
! an XW value field to +0 -- IBM bit 24 is the sign (2**39) and bit 45
! is the low bit of count (2**18).
        LX, $X1, WNEGZ               ! value -0, count 1
        BZXVZ, $                     ! hang up if XVZ off -- value IS zero
        BXVLZ, $                     ! hang up if XVLZ on -- zero is not
                                     !   "non-zero and negative"
        BXVGZ, $                     ! hang up if XVGZ on
        B, PASS
WNEG    XW, -1, 0, 0, 1              ! ja_ax1a's IBXWZO
WPOS    XW, 1, 1, 0, 0               ! ja_ax1a's IBXWZ2
WZERO   XW, 0, 1, 0, 0               ! ja_ax1a's IBXWZ1
WFLAG   XW, 1, 1, 0, 1               ! flag 1 with a NON-zero count
WNOFLG  XW, 1, 0, 0, 0               ! flag 0 with a zero count
WNEGZ   DD(BU,64), 549756076032      ! value -0 (sign bit 24), count 1
PASS    BEW, $
        END, START
